      "claim": "Let x be the number of students in the smallest group.",
      "evidence": "Definition of variable based on the problem statement identifying the smallest group.",
      "weight": 0.8,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The number of students in each of the two equal groups is x + 10.",
      "evidence": "The problem states the smallest group is 10 less than each of the other groups, implying the other groups are 10 more than the smallest.",
      "weight": 0.9,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The total number of students is the sum of the students in all three groups: x + (x + 10) + (x + 10).",
      "evidence": "Addition of the group sizes derived in steps 1 and 2.",
      "weight": 0.9,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The equation for the total number of students is 3x + 20 = 200.",
      "evidence": "Simplifying the expression from step 3 (x + x + 10 + x + 10 = 3x + 20) and equating it to the total class size of 200.",
      "weight": 1.0,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Solving for x gives 3x = 180.",
      "evidence": "Subtracting 20 from both sides of the equation 3x + 20 = 200.",
      "weight": 1.0,
      "depends_on": [4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Dividing by 3 gives x = 60.",
      "evidence": "Dividing both sides of 3x = 180 by 3.",
      "weight": 1.0,
      "depends_on": [5]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "60",
  "confidence": 1.0,
